Use a `setTimeout` for debouncing and an `AbortController` for cancelling the previous request. Clear both in the effect cleanup.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const query = q.trim();

    // Optionally clear results for empty queries.
    if (!query) {
      setResults([]);
      setError(null);
      return;
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();

    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`, {
        signal: controller.signal,
      })
        .then((response) => {
          if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
          }
          return response.json();
        })
        .then(setResults)
        .catch((err) => {
          // AbortError is expected when a request is cancelled/superseded.
          if (err.name !== 'AbortError') {
            setError(err.message);
          }
        });
    }, 300);

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [q]);

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={q}
        onChange={(e) => setQ(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Search..."
      />

      {error ? <p>Error: {error}</p> : null}

      <ul>
        {results.map((r) => (
          <li key={r.id}>{r.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

What this does:

- Every time `q` changes, the previous effect cleanup runs.
- `clearTimeout` cancels the pending debounce.
- `controller.abort()` cancels any in-flight fetch from the previous effect.
- The request only runs after the user stops typing for 300ms.
- The `AbortError` catch branch avoids showing errors for cancelled requests.

This also cancels correctly on unmount.

